   268	`corr=0`) has `a=1`, `2a=2<=3`: Lemma 3.4 returns `D ≅ A^1`, which is exactly
   269	what Orevkov obtains at `jc86.pdf` p. 10 from his Lemma 5.3 — an independent
   270	re-derivation of a step he proves by a different (separation) argument.
   271	(ii) The surviving `N=4` profile `a=2` with one dicritical of `(mu,corr)=(2,1)`
   272	satisfies neither (i) nor (ii) of Lemma 3.4 — as it must not, since that profile
   273	is not excluded here.
   274	
   275	### 3.4 The main irreducible theorem
   276	
   277	> **Theorem 3.5.** Let `F` be a noninvertible Keller map of geometric degree
   278	> `N >= 3` with `A_F` **irreducible**, `a := a_{A_F}`. Then neither of the
   279	> following can hold: (i) `A_F` is smooth; (ii) every `corr_l = 0` and
   280	> `2a <= N`.
   281	> *Proof.* Under either hypothesis Lemma 3.4 gives `a=1` and `D := A_F ≅ A^1`,
   282	> a smooth closed curve in `C^2`, so by [AMS] there is `alpha ∈ Aut(C^2)` with
   283	> `alpha(D)` a line; hence `C^2 - D ≅ C × C^*` and `pi_1(C^2-D) ≅ Z`, generated
   284	> by one meridian `gamma`. `Br != ∅` (Lemma 3.1) and `Br ⊆ A_F = D` irreducible,
   285	> so `Br = D` and `q` is finite étale of degree `N` over `C^2-D` with connected
   286	> total space. By Lemma 3.2 the monodromy image `<rho(gamma)>` is transitive on
   287	> `N` letters; a cyclic transitive subgroup of `S_N` is generated by an
   288	> `N`-cycle, so `rho(gamma)` is an `N`-cycle and has no fixed point. But its
   289	> cycle type is `{mu_l^{(s_l)}} ∪ {1^{a}}`, with `a` fixed points, so `a = 0`,
   290	> contradicting the promoted `a >= 1`. ∎
   291	
   292	> **Corollary 3.6 (irreducible `A_F` is singular).** For a noninvertible Keller
   293	> map, `A_F` cannot be irreducible and smooth.
   294	
   295	This is a normalisation-free strengthening of [C-4.4] in the irreducible case:
   296	Chau's proof that every component of `E_f` is singular runs through
   297	Abhyankar–Moh applied to a *regular embedding* and needs the Jung form
   298	`d>e>1`; Theorem 3.5(i) needs no Jung normalisation and no `deg P/deg Q`
   299	hypothesis. It also independently reproves the promoted "smooth-`A_F` law"
   300	and upgrades it from `a=1` to outright impossibility.
   301	
   302	> **Corollary 3.7 (Orevkov's inequality is strict under H2).** If `A_F` is
   303	> irreducible then `sum_{l} mu_l <= N-2`; equivalently `sum_l corr_l >= 1`;
   304	> equivalently (by [Z-6.5b]) `A_F` carries at least one **non-immersive** point,
   305	> i.e. a locally irreducible singularity of some `Phi(l')`.
   306	> *Proof.* Suppose `sum_l mu_l = N-1`. By [O-4.2] Cor. 4.3 this is exactly the
   307	> equality case, so every `corr_l = 0`. All dicriticals map onto the irreducible
   308	> `A_F`, so `a = N - sum_l s_l mu_l <= N - sum_l mu_l = 1` (using `s_l >= 1`),
   309	> and `a >= 1` forces `a = 1` and `s_l = 1` for every `l`. Then `2a = 2 <= N`,
   310	> and Theorem 3.5(ii) applies. ∎
   311	
   312	> **Corollary 3.8 (Orevkov's closing Remark, reproved).** No component of `L_F`
   313	> has `mu_l f^* = N-1`.
   314	> *Proof.* If `mu_{l_1} = N-1` then [O-4.2] forces every other term of the budget
   315	> to vanish, so `l_1` is the only dicritical and `corr_{l_1}=0`; hence `A_F` is
   316	> irreducible and `sum_l mu_l = N-1`, contradicting Cor. 3.7. ∎
   317	
   318	Corollary 3.8 recovers, with a complete proof, the assertion Orevkov states as
   319	an unproved Remark ("repeating the above arguments…", `jc86.pdf` p. 10) and that
   320	Domrina–Orevkov cite as `[3, remark after 5.3]` (`do.pdf` p. 6). It is a
