   325	## 4. Route 3 — Unique-versus-mixed at N=4
   326	
   327	### 4.1 The complete profile list
   328	
   329	By [O-4.2] the budget is an **equality**: `sum_l (mu_l + corr_l) = N-1 = 3`,
   330	each summand `>= 1`. Writing profiles as multisets of pairs `(mu_l, corr_l)`,
   331	the complete list at `N=4` is
   332	
   333	| # dicriticals | profile | contains `mu=1`? |
   334	|---|---|---|
   335	| 1 | `(3,0)` | no |
   336	| 1 | `(2,1)` | no |
   337	| 1 | `(1,2)` | **yes** — (c) |
   338	| 2 | `(2,0)+(1,0)` | **yes** — (d) |
   339	| 2 | `(1,1)+(1,0)` | **yes** — (e) |
   340	| 3 | `(1,0)+(1,0)+(1,0)` | **yes** — (f) |
   341	
   342	Row `(3,0)` is excluded outright by Cor. 3.8 (Orevkov's Remark, here proved).
   343	Row `(2,1)` carries no trivial dicritical and is the profile that survives all
   344	arguments in this report; it is the promoted `a=2`, `(delta,e)=(1,2)`,
   345	`b=0` row of SHEET-GATE Thm 4.4.
   346	
   347	### 4.2 Rows (c), (e), (f): killed by the branch locus
   348	
   349	> **Proposition 4.1.** Profiles (c), (e), (f) are impossible.
   350	> *Proof.* In each of them every dicritical has `mu_l = 1`, so `Br = ∅` in the
   351	> sense of Lemma 3.1 — and Lemma 3.1 gives `N = 1`. ∎
   352	
   353	This is the numbered replacement the brief asked for. Domrina–Orevkov dismiss the
   354	unique-`mu=1` case (our row (c)) with the sentence "If the ramification order is
   355	1 then `F` is an unbranched covering over `C^2`. This is also impossible."
   356	(`do.pdf` p. 6, §2). As the registry flagged (R12), that sentence is **not** a
   357	verbatim Orevkov theorem: at `N=4` a unique `mu=1` dicritical is forced by
   358	[O-4.2] to carry `corr = 2`, so `f^*` is *not* unbranched over all of `C^2` —
   359	it is unbranched only over the complement of one or two points. Their conclusion
   360	is nevertheless correct, by either of two complete arguments: topologically,
   361	`C^2` minus a finite set is still simply connected, so a connected `4`-fold
   362	covering of it is impossible; algebraically, Prop. 4.1 above, which never
   363	mentions the correction points at all because purity of the branch locus
   364	(Zariski–Nagata) rules out isolated branch points a priori. **The author
   365	inference flagged at R12/Chain C is hereby discharged.**
   366	
   367	### 4.3 Row (d): the mixed remainder
   368	
   369	Row (d) is the transposition-horn case. Its numerics: one dicritical `l_1` with
   370	`mu_1 = 2`, one `l_0` with `mu_0 = 1`, and `corr_1 = corr_0 = 0`, so [O-5.2] and
   371	Lemma 3.3 apply to **both**: every branch of `Phi(l_1')` and of `Phi(l_0')` at
   372	every affine point is smooth. That is exactly the rigidity the brief asked for:
   373	`corr_l = 0` at a trivial dicritical means the parametrisation `l_0' -> D_0` is
   374	an **immersion with no critical point whatsoever**, so `D_0` has no
   375	locally-irreducible singularity, and likewise for `D_1`.
   376	
   377	> **Theorem 4.2 (`N=4` under H2).** Let `F` be a noninvertible Keller map of
   378	> geometric degree `4` with `A_F` **irreducible**. Then `F` has no dicritical
   379	> with affine image and `mu_l = 1`. Consequently `b = 0`, `a = 2`, the boundary
   380	> profile is `(delta, e) = (1,2)`, the meridian is a transposition with
   381	> `G = S_4`, and **(M) holds verbatim at rank four in the transposition class**.
   382	> *Proof.* Rows (c), (e), (f) die by Prop. 4.1. Row (d) with `A_F` irreducible
   383	> has every `corr_l = 0`, hence `sum_l mu_l = 3 = N-1`, contradicting Cor. 3.7.
   384	> Row `(3,0)` dies by Cor. 3.8. Only `(2,1)` survives; over the irreducible
   385	> `A_F`, `a + s_1·2 = 4` with `a >= 1` gives `s_1 = 1`, `a = 2`, `b = 0`. ∎
   386	
   387	Theorem 4.2 **refutes the SHEET-GATE Thm 4.4 row `a=1`, `(1,2)+(1,1)`, `b=1`
   388	under H2**, which is the entire content of the charged transposition horn once
   389	`A_F` is known irreducible. It also removes the dependence on the (unproved)
   390	census equality `f(z)=2`: Cor. 4.5 of the SHEET-GATE report is no longer needed.
   391	
   392	### 4.4 Row (d) without H2: the residual configuration
   393	
   394	If `A_F` is *reducible*, row (d) survives with the two dicriticals over
   395	**different** components. Everything about it can be pinned:
   396	
   397	> **Theorem 4.3 (structure of the residual configuration).** Suppose a
   398	> noninvertible Keller map of degree `4` has a dicritical with affine image and
   399	> `mu = 1`. Then, with `D_1 := Phi(l_1')`, `D_0 := Phi(l_0')`:
   400	> 1. `F` has exactly two dicriticals with affine image, `(mu_1,corr_1)=(2,0)`
   401	>    and `(mu_0,corr_0)=(1,0)`; `A_F = D_1 ∪ D_0` with `D_1 != D_0`.
   402	> 2. `s_1 = 1`, `a_{D_1} = 2`; `s_0 ∈ {1,2,3}`, `a_{D_0} = 4 - s_0`.
   403	> 3. `Br = D_1`. `q` is finite étale of degree `4` over `C^2 - D_1` with
   404	>    connected total space, so `pi_1(C^2 - D_1)` surjects onto a transitive
   405	>    subgroup of `S_4` generated by conjugates of one transposition, i.e. onto
   406	>    `S_4`. In particular `pi_1(C^2 - D_1)` is **non-abelian**.
   407	> 4. Every affine branch of `D_1` and of `D_0` is smooth. Every `p ∈ Sing D_1`
   408	>    with `p ∉ D_0` has exactly two branches, `a_p = 0`, and local monodromy
   409	>    generated by two **disjoint** transpositions (cycle type `(2,2)`).
   410	> 5. Writing `D~_1 = P^1 - {r_1 pts}` (`= l_1'`, since `s_1=1`),
   411	>    `D~_0 = P^1 - {c_0 pts}`, `k_p` the number of branches of `D_0` at `p`,
   412	>    `t_p` the number of points of `l_0'` over `p`, and `eps_p >= 0` the number
   413	>    of contracted (`L_C`) fibre points over `p`, the aggregate Euler identity is
   414	>    ```
   415	>      2 r_1 + s_0 c_0 + sum_{p ∈ Sing A_F} (s_0 k_p - t_p) + sum_p eps_p
   416	>          = 1 + 2 s_0 ,
   417	>    ```
   418	>    all displayed summands being `>= 0`. Hence **`c_0 = 1`, i.e.
   419	>    `D~_0 ≅ A^1`**, and `r_1 = 1` (so `D~_1 ≅ A^1`) except possibly when
   420	>    `s_0 = 3`, where `r_1 = 2` (`D~_1 ≅ C^*`) is also numerically admissible.
   421	>
   422	> *Proof.* (1) is the budget plus §4.2. (2): over generic `p ∈ D_1`,
   423	> `a_{D_1} + 2 s_1 = 4` with `a_{D_1} >= 1` forces `s_1 = 1`, `a_{D_1} = 2`;
   424	> over generic `p ∈ D_0`, `a_{D_0} + s_0 = 4`. (3): Lemma 3.1 with `Br = D_1`;
   425	> transitivity and generation from Lemma 3.2; `S_4` is the only transitive
   426	> subgroup of `S_4` generated by transpositions. (4): Lemma 3.3 for smoothness;
   427	> at `p ∈ Sing D_1 - D_0` the fibre relation `2 beta_p + a_p + eps_p = 4` with
   428	> `beta_p >= 2` (two smooth branches, `l_1' ≅ D~_1`) forces `beta_p = 2`,
   429	> `a_p = eps_p = 0`; the local monodromy orbits then have sizes `(2,2)`, and two
   430	> transpositions generating a group with orbit sizes `2,2` are disjoint.
   431	> (5): substitute `chi_i = chi_c(D~_i) - n_i` into the aggregate identity
   432	> `sum_i (4-a_i) chi_i = 3 - 4 s + sum_p a_p` (proved as in Lemma 3.4, with
   433	> `D_i^0 = D_i - Sing A_F`), and eliminate `sum_p a_p` using the three fibre
   434	> relations `2 beta_p + t_p + a_p + eps_p = 4` (`beta_p = 2, t_p = 0` on
   435	> `Sing D_1 - D_0`; `beta_p = 1` on `D_1 ∩ D_0`; `beta_p = 0` on
   436	> `Sing D_0 - D_1`) together with `n_1 = sum_p beta_p`, `n_2 = sum_p k_p`. The
   437	> boundary points over `A_F - Sing A_F` are exactly the `s_l` dicritical points
   438	> by the promoted covering lemma, so `eps_p = 0` off `Sing A_F`. Every term
   439	> `s_0 k_p - t_p` is `>= 0` because `l_0' -> D~_0` has degree `s_0`. The
   440	> stated bounds follow from `r_1 >= 1`, `c_0 >= 1` by inspection of
   441	> `s_0 ∈ {1,2,3}`. ∎
   442	
   443	> **Corollary 4.4.** In the configuration of Thm. 4.3, if `r_1 = 1` — which by
   444	> part (5) holds whenever `s_0 ∈ {1,2}`, and in the `s_0=3` case unless
   445	> `D~_1 ≅ C^*` — then `D_1` is **singular**, and `Sing D_1` is a nonempty set of
   446	> double points of two smooth branches at which no affine preimage survives.
   447	> *Proof.* If `D_1` were smooth its normalisation map would be an isomorphism,
   448	> so `D_1 ≅ P^1 - {1 pt} = A^1`, a smooth closed curve in `C^2`; [AMS] would
   449	> rectify it and give `pi_1(C^2 - D_1) ≅ Z`, contradicting Thm. 4.3(3). The
   450	> description of `Sing D_1` is Thm. 4.3(4). ∎
   451	
   452	Theorem 4.3 is exactly the "surviving configuration" the registry's Route 2
   453	predicted (`mu=1` onto a curve that is *not* smooth-embedded), sharpened: the
   454	trivial dicritical's own image `D_0` is forced to be a **polynomial curve**
   455	(`D~_0 ≅ A^1`), all of whose affine branches are smooth; and the obstruction has
   456	migrated entirely onto the *other* component `D_1`, where it is a
   457	fundamental-group question and nothing else.
