   475	    To complete the proof of the lemma, it remains to note that by [6], Corollary 10.2,
   476	the commutator subgroup of G is a free group with an even number of generators.
   477	Thus, by Lemma 5.1, the knot K is trivial, and hence, by a theorem of Zariski [7],
   478	the corresponding branch of the curve f ∗ (l∗ ) is nonsingular. The lemma is proved.
   479	Lemma 5.3. Suppose that N > 2, l = LF (see §2) is irreducible, and µl f = N − 1.
   480	                                 def
   481	Then the restriction of f to l′ = l − L∞ is a biregular isomorphism, and hence, the
   482	curve f (l′ ) is nonsingular in C2 .
   483	Proof. The mapping f |l′ separates points. Indeed, if f (x) = f (y) for distinct points
   484	x, y ∈ l′ , then µx f ∗ + µy f ∗ = 2N − 2 > N which contradicts the condition (2) of
   485	constant multiplicity of f . Hence, first, f (l′ ) is nonsingular since it may not have
   486	irreducible singularities by Lemma 5.2, and second, f |l′ is an isomorphism. The
   487	lemma is proved.
   488	   In order to prove Theorem 1.1, we shall show that the multiplicity N of f ∗ (see
   489	§4) cannot equal two or three. The fact that N 6= 2 was proved in §1, but we may
   490	also derive it as an immediate corollary of Lemma 4.3. If N = 2 then, by Corollary
   491	4.3 the multiplicity of f ∗ at every point of π(LF ) except the point ∞ is equal to
   492	one, hence, f ∗ is a two-fold unbranched covering of C2 , which is a contradiction.
   493	   Suppose that N = 3. Then by Lemma 4.2 three cases may occur: 1) LF consists
   494	of two components and µx f ∗ = 1 for all x ∈ π(LF ) − {∞}; 2) LF is irreducible
   495	and µx f ∗ = 1 for all x ∈ π(LF ) − {∞, x0 }, where x0 6= ∞; or 3) LF is irreducible
   496	10                                   S.YU. OREVKOV
   497	
   498	and µx f ∗ = 2 for all x ∈ π(LF ) − {∞}. However, the first two cases are impossible
   499	because otherwise f ∗ would be an unbranched three-fold covering of C2 or of C2 −
   500	f ∗ (x0 ) which contradicts the fact that these varieties are simply connected.
   501	    So we assume that the case 3) holds. Then by Lemma 5.3 the curve f (LF )∩C2 is
   502	isomorphic to C1 and we may apply the following theorem of Abhyankar and Moh
   503	[8]: If l is a curve in C2 which is isomorphic to C1 then there exists a polynomial
   504	automorphism α : C2 → C2 such that α(l) is a line. Therefore, there space M =
   505	C2 − f (LF ) is homotopically equivalent to a circle, and this implies that there
   506	exists a unique (up to equivalence) three-fold unbranched covering of M . It may
   507	be uniquely extended up to a branched covering of C2 . It is easy to see that
   508	the branching order of this covering along f (LF ) is equal to three. However, we
   509	assumed that µl f ∗ = 2. This contradiction completes the proof of the theorem.
   510	Remark. Repeating the above arguments, one can prove the following assertion. If
   511	f : C2 → C2 is a polynomial mapping satisfying condition (1), then the curve LF
   512	(see §2) cannot contain an irreducible component l such that µl f ∗ = N − 1.
